trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Occupational, chemical, cosmetic, and travel-related exposures are absent — making exogenous or infectious causes (e.g., chemical leukoderma, tinea versicolor, leprosy) much less likely. The clinical picture now strongly favors an intrinsic, likely autoimmune pigmentary disorder — vitiligo remains top differential. Before proceeding to physical exam and testing, I must complete the evidence framework by obtaining targeted physical examination findings: distribution pattern (e.g., acrofacial, segmental, generalized), symmetry, border characteristics (sharp vs. ill-defined), presence of Koebner phenomenon (new lesions at trauma sites), and involvement of mucosa or hair-bearing skin — all essential for classification and diagnosis.

[TEST] Perform a full-skin examination with attention to:  
- Distribution and symmetry of depigmented patches (e.g., peri-orificial, acral, truncal, segmental);  
- Border definition (sharp, scalloped, or ill-defined);  
- Presence of perilesional hyperpigmentation or inflammation;  
- Involvement of mucous membranes (lips, oral cavity, genitalia);  
- Hair within depigmented patches (color, texture);  
- Evidence of Koebner phenomenon (new hypopigmented lesions at sites of prior minor trauma).

